{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>If your brother, your mother's son, entices you, etc.</b> The rashei tevot of כי יסיתך have the gematria of ביחוד — an allusion to the unity from the Torah." "<b>like your own soul</b> Two times in the Masorah: אשר כנפשך. כנפשך שבעך. This is as stated in Ḥullin: there is no enticement through words — but is it not written כי יסיתך? That too is through eating and drinking, and that is כנפשך like כנפשך שבעך — this is eating." "<b>which you have not known, you and your fathers</b> It is written regarding the mesit (enticer) — because 'your friend who is like your own soul,' i.e., his father entices him, to say: you shall not learn to do from your fathers, who is your father's father — rather you shall learn from your father."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {2 "The reading 'which you have not known' follows the verse's wording; the comment turns on the masoretic juxtaposition of this phrase with the mesit passage."}, :source-witnesses [["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"]], :timestamp "2026-07-12T19:09:41.009333837Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}